{
  "summary": "The third proposal's 'two-line fix' has three gaps. First, the found_local branch fix is under-specified: find_config() tracks found_local as a boolean but doesn't record WHICH directory contained the .mad/ folder. When found_local is True and no config.json exists, ensure_local_config() needs a target directory — but the proposal implies calling it for 'that directory' without tracking it. The loop would need to save the closest .mad/ parent path (e.g., found_local_dir) and pass it to ensure_local_config(found_local_dir), not ensure_local_config(cwd), because the .mad/ directory may be in a parent and creating a config in cwd would produce a second .mad/ at the wrong level. Second, the walk-up loop (lines 60-69) stops at home or root, but ~/MAD itself is under home — so if cwd is ~/chickentracks.app and ~/MAD/.mad/ exists (it will, since that's the global install), the loop will find ~/MAD/.mad/ as found_local=True before hitting the home stop condition. This means found_local will almost ALWAYS be True for any directory under ~, making the 'not found_local' branch at line 72 nearly unreachable — the real problem isn't that found_local prevents the None return, it's that the loop traverses too far up and treats ~/MAD/.mad/ as a 'local' .mad directory. The fix needs to either stop traversal at a sensible boundary (e.g., stop at first .mad/ found, or never traverse above the project root) or distinguish between 'local project .mad/' and 'global ~/MAD/.mad/'. Third, the proposal's ensure_local_config() in install_service() only covers the systemd code path — a user running 'pipeline tui' directly from ~/chickentracks.app would still hit the same global fallback. The fix must be in find_config() itself, not just in install_service()."
}
